00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:42.462
Let me get right into point number one.

00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:43.462
Thick skin.

00:00:43.462 --> 00:00:44.990
What do you mean by thick skin?

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.170
Let me explain it to you.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:53.680
There is something that happens when I grow
up in a very peaceful family with no problems.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:54.860
Everything's covered.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:55.990
Things are good.

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:57.079
Things are smooth.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:00:59.090
Mom and dad never argue.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:00.090
Everything's just chill.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.880
You know those families that they talk and
just everything is easy going.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.820
Now I want you to think about the other family.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:08.310
Imagine you're in a family when you're escaping
a war.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:10.369
You're getting bombed on every week.

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:13.090
Where your kids can't go play outside because
everybody's afraid.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:16.359
Where mom and dad are afraid to go outside
and tension is higher.

00:01:16.359 --> 00:01:17.619
You're working longer hours.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:19.539
You ain't got time around your kids.

00:01:19.539 --> 00:01:20.590
Your temper's shorter.

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:22.060
You got a shorter fuse.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.869
You get upset at the kids a little bit faster.

00:01:23.869 --> 00:01:25.409
You give them a look.

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:26.450
You push the kids around.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.280
Parents have all this pressure on them and
they have to release it somewhere.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.369
If the wife doesn't see her husband five,
six days a week because he's out there working

00:01:33.369 --> 00:01:37.229
and the only thing he sees or she sees are
the kids, she has to release that tension

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:38.229
somewhere.

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:39.229
Where else is it going to go?

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:40.319
The kids gets it, right?

00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:42.429
Now some people will only say, "That's called
bad parenting."

00:01:42.429 --> 00:01:46.240
Unless if you've never been in a situation
like that, you can't say what it's like because

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.240
it's tough.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.240
What happens there?

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.909
There's a big benefit to it.

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:51.639
Here's what the benefit is.

00:01:51.639 --> 00:01:57.180
That kid says, "If I can handle living with
my mommy and my daddy, guess what?

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:02.299
I can handle anybody else because my skin
is so thick that your little rejection saying

00:02:02.299 --> 00:02:07.219
no to my proposal means nothing, nothing,
because I can live in this family with this

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:08.219
mommy and daddy.

00:02:08.219 --> 00:02:09.569
I can definitely handle you."

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:13.120
When you get thick skin, the world of business
is so easy for you because you're not worried

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.540
about somebody hurting your feelings.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.249
Number two, patience.

00:02:17.249 --> 00:02:20.400
In the world of business, you're going to
need a lot of patience.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:28.000
I remember living in Iran 1984 and my dad
and my mom finally deciding to apply for a

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.780
green card to come to America.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.500
We would watch Rocky in 1985 when it came
out.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.379
We'll watch all these movies about United
States.

00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:36.379
This is heaven.

00:02:36.379 --> 00:02:37.379
People were coming.

00:02:37.379 --> 00:02:39.290
They were celebrating about coming to America.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:40.290
They got their number.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.590
They got their free ticket to come to the
land of opportunities.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.709
From 1984 we waited until '89.

00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:47.709
Khomeini dies.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:50.560
We don't have the green card yet.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.920
Khomeini dies June 3rd.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:53.630
Six weeks later we escape and we go to Germany.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.719
I lived in a refugee camp in Germany for a
year and a half.

00:02:55.719 --> 00:02:57.739
Then we finally get our papers.

00:02:57.739 --> 00:03:00.640
Then we come to America November 28, 1990.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:04.620
Took seven and a half years until we finally
made it to the States.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.800
It requires a lot of patience until you get
what you want, same thing with business.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.640
Number three, long hours.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.349
Nine to five is an American thing.

00:03:14.349 --> 00:03:16.990
There is not really a lot of nine to five
going on with immigrants.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:20.370
Immigrants wake up in the morning and they
work until they have their money on the table,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.500
food on the table, to go buy stuff, pay rent,
all this stuff with their kids.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.799
There is no nine to five.

00:03:24.799 --> 00:03:27.811
It's not like a nine to five in all these
other things you're dealing with.

00:03:27.811 --> 00:03:31.349
You get to work and you come home and you
make sure everything is covered.

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:34.510
Typically, they're used to working six days
a week, not five days a week.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.420
In America, they're trying to go to four days
a week.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.099
They're even trying to write a book called
"4-Hour Workweek" by a guy named Tim Ferriss.

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:42.099
It's like work less.

00:03:42.099 --> 00:03:44.549
Immigrants say, "Hey, everybody in America.

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:45.989
You guys keep working four hours a week.

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:48.549
We're gong to bust our behinds and guess what?

00:03:48.549 --> 00:03:52.580
40% of Fortune 500 companies will be founded
by us and our kids, but you guys keep working

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:53.650
four hours a week.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:56.510
Leave that market for us because we're not
afraid of hard work."

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:57.510
They bring that to the table.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:02.209
In the world of business, if you ain't afraid
of this, everyone's going to eventually find

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:04.700
out about your business if you're not afraid
of this.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.420
Number four, they value money a little bit
more.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:12.850
Here's why, I remember when we were living
in Germany at a refugee camp and I wanted

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.430
to go to a movie, I don't think we ever went
to movies in Germany.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:19.100
I don't think we ever went to movies in Germany.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:24.430
If we even had a chance to go somewhere and
do anything with it, it was like magical.

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.320
I remember being 11 years old.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.960
We played the first lotto.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.600
I find out about lotto.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:33.060
I had saved up five bucks and I went and played
lotto.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.290
Sometimes people ask me how come I don't play
the lottery.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:37.570
I went and played five bucks and I picked
these numbers.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:38.670
I remember the numbers still today.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.030
44 was one of them.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.390
60 was another one.

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:42.390
38 was another.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:45.040
I picked my mom's age at that time, my dad's
age at that time.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.040
I came and I said, "Oh my god.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.630
I don't know why I feel like I'm going to
win and we're going to be millionaires.

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:51.010
My mom and dad, everybody's going to be good.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.720
We're going to be taken care of.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.040
This is going to change our lives."

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.040
Boom.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.040
We didn't win.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.480
I cried all night like a little baby.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:58.530
You know why?

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.060
Because you know how much was five marks to
our family?

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.470
Man, it was a lot of money what five marks
was.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:09.040
When you come to the States, you value money
a little bit more because you work your tail

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.330
for this money so you understand we ain't
wasting this money here, kid.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:16.930
We ain't buying all these nonsense stuff because
you value money a little bit more.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:20.190
In the world of business, the more you can
know how to value, you know how to stretch

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.840
that dollar, your business lasts a lot longer.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.230
Number five, they're used to taking risks.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.470
Uncertainly and risk is part of the life.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.050
I mean it depends on what part of the country
and world you come from.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:33.380
I always like to travel and go different places
and ask questions.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:34.380
What is it like living here?

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.270
Tell me about what the daily income people
make?

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:39.270
What is the income here?

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.270
We were in Cancun.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:42.340
Tell me what people are making here?

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:43.340
I'm asking.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:44.840
Tell me about what it's like.

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:47.620
They're so used to risk and uncertainty.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.970
They're so used to risk and uncertainty.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.060
It's like everyday is a risk.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:53.590
Everyday is an uncertainty.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:55.240
In the world of business, it's the same thing.

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:59.360
By the way, most people cannot handle too
much risk and too much uncertainty.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:01.870
Most people like a lot of things to be safe.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:03.000
They don't like too much risk.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.810
They don't like too much uncertainty.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:08.180
That's why if you're going to get into the
business world and you don't know how to handle

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:09.680
this, all of a sudden it's a shell shock.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:10.680
I can't handle this.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:13.900
You go back to what you were doing before,
but immigrants already know how to handle

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:14.900
that.

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.210
Number six, sacrifice.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.630
Sacrifice could be on a lot of different skills
by the way.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:25.220
Time away from family, time away from partying,
time away from fooling around, so many different

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:26.220
things.

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.890
This is a must in the world of business.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.780
It's a must for what immigrants do.

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:33.150
Imagine if you're already used to going to
the places you eat, churches you go to, families

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:37.660
you visit, friends, all this stuff you're
accustomed to living around for 5, 10, 15,

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:38.860
20, 30, 40 years.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:41.840
Then all of a sudden you pack your bags and
leave the country and go to a new place and

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:42.840
you don't speak the language.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.770
You know how many sacrifices you're making?

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:45.770
You're used to it.

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:48.060
In the world business, they make easy transitions.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:53.120
A big sacrifice to somebody else could be
a small sacrifice to an immigrant entrepreneur.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.330
Number seven, another level of commonalities
because of language.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:58.580
Think about it.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.611
Imagine if me, I remember when I joined the
army, I went to PX.

00:07:01.611 --> 00:07:04.180
PX is military's Walmart.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:11.520
I went to PX and I ran into everybody at Jackson,
South Carolina at the time.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:14.720
Everybody was Caucasian, African-America,
Hispanic.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:15.890
Here I am walking.

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:19.570
I go to PX and I see another guy with a crooked
nose.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:20.570
I said, "Wait a minute.

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:21.820
I recognize crooked noses."

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.390
Middle Easterns we got this crooked noses.

00:07:23.390 --> 00:07:25.200
I said, "Hey man, where you from?"

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.860
He says, "Where are you from?"

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:27.900
I said, "I'm from Glendale.

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.440
I'm half Armenian, half Farsi."

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:30.660
He says, "No way.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:31.660
I'm from Glendale.

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:32.950
You got to be kidding me."

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:33.950
Boom.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:34.950
We connected.

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:35.950
There's a relationship.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.130
If we don't speak that language, we don't
have that connection.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:41.510
It's a whole different story than friends
that also speak Armenian and Syrian.

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.250
I get connected to people that speak Farsi.

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.860
I get to connected to that because there's
another language you speak.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.710
If you do business with somebody that speaks
Spanish, you can't get into the community

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:50.710
of people that speak Spanish.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:53.590
I recently sat with a guy here who's from
Pakistan.

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:57.300
I said, "What percent is your businesses with
people who are Pakistani?"

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.140
He says 95%.

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:04.070
He can connect with an audience that you may
not be able to connect with because he is

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.040
part of that community, he's part of that
culture.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.120
It helps in business as well.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.420
Culturally, language, it helps.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:13.160
Number eight, help businesses go global.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:18.150
What's this Red Bull guy stories with Dietrich,
some strange last name.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:19.410
The founder of Red Bull.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:20.790
Interesting story.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:25.780
He apparently goes to Thailand and in Thailand
they're making this drink, it's called Krav

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:32.710
Daeng or something like that, Krav Daeng which
in the language means red bull.

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:33.710
He says, "My gosh.

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:34.710
What is this drink made for?"

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.780
He says, "For truck drivers so people don't
fall asleep."

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:37.840
He drinks it.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:38.840
He likes it.

00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:40.250
He brings it to America.

00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:47.460
He started selling it and puts Red Bull on
it and now he's worth $15.7 billion on a drink

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.110
called Red Bull.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:54.560
You know how many billions of Red Bulls you
have to sell to have a net worth of $15.7

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:55.560
billion?

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:56.560
That's how many he sold.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:57.560
What happened?

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:59.250
Globally connected.

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:02.000
He knows because he's connected to other places.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.140
You're constantly in another country.

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:05.880
You're connected with other people.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.940
You're not worried about going to another
country because you're already used to it.

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.090
He brings that idea back.

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.730
It helps the business out here in America
and all over the world because everybody now

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:16.830
... Not everybody, but billions of Red Bulls
have been sold all over the world.

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:20.030
Number nine, ownership matters to them.

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:22.620
Why does ownership matter to them?

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:27.050
We had a story of ... This happened where
we were at, but you here this in different

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:30.030
types of countries as well where you own a
property, all of a sudden the government comes

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:33.490
and takes the property away from you because
hey, we have a new law.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:34.490
This is our land.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:35.490
You can't do nothing about it.

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:36.490
Here you go.

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:38.540
This is what you got, one-fifth of what the
value is.

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:39.540
Hush.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:40.540
I stay quiet or else.

00:09:40.540 --> 00:09:41.540
Boom.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:42.540
Give us the property.

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:43.540
It's gone.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:44.540
Right?

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:47.670
That idea of somebody taking away your home
from you or your land from you, it's like

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:49.100
somebody's saying, "Hey, that's my girl.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:50.100
Give me your girl.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:51.100
It's my wife.

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:52.100
Give me your wife.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.100
That's my wife.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.100
What are you talking about?

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:55.100
It's my wife.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.100
No.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:57.100
No.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:58.100
No.

00:09:58.100 --> 00:09:59.100
It's my wife after now.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:00.100
Here's 10 grand.

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:01.100
Walk away.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:02.100
Don't complain.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:03.100
She's my wife now.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:04.100
The kind of stuff that happens when you ... That's
my wife.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.100
That's my husband.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:06.100
That's my ownership.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:07.400
In the world of business, immigrants love
ownership because they're all about freedom.

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:08.610
I want to have control.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:09.610
This is mine.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:10.610
I worked my butt off.

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:11.610
Leave me alone.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.730
Let me get to work and let me have the life
that I want to have.

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:16.990
Let me have the family that I want to have
because ownership matters.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:18.380
That's why they like being entrepreneurs.

